+# The Daily Grind
+
+A coffee shop homepage with passwordless accounts at `/account`.
+
+## Run
+
+Requires Node.js 24 or newer and pnpm.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+pnpm build
+pnpm start
+```
+
+Open `http://localhost:11222`. `PORT` overrides the port. The generated client bundle is committed, so normal starts do not require a build.
+
+For production, set `NODE_ENV=production` and `APP_ORIGIN=https://your-domain.example`. Terminate HTTPS at your reverse proxy and forward requests to the Node server. `APP_ORIGIN` must be the exact public origin, including any nonstandard port; its hostname is the passkey relying party ID. Without this setting, local development derives the origin from the request host. Use a stable domain: passkeys cannot be transferred between relying party IDs. WebAuthn requires HTTPS except on localhost.
+
+`DATABASE_PATH` defaults to `data/accounts.sqlite`. Mount a persistent volume there and back up the database. SQLite stores users, public credential material, counters, metadata, and hashed session tokens; no private passkey keys or biometrics reach the server. The database and sessions persist across restarts. This deployment uses a single Node process and local SQLite, with per-process IP rate limiting; horizontal deployment needs a shared database and rate-limit store.
+
+## Account flows
+
+- Create an account with a unique username, display name, and discoverable passkey. Usernames are identifiers, not verified email addresses. User records are created only after the passkey is verified.
+- Sign in through the explicit passkey button or conditional browser autofill. The username field is optional because discoverable passkeys identify the account.
+- List passkeys, see creation and last-used dates, rename them, or add a backup passkey on another device or security key. Removing the last passkey is blocked to prevent lockout. Accounts are passkey-only: save a second passkey before losing access to the first; there is no password or email recovery flow.
+- Verify identity with an existing account passkey before adding/removing passkeys or changing profile details. A server-checked verification grant lasts five minutes. Sign-ins without user verification are accepted but do not grant sensitive access.
+- Supported browsers receive password-manager signals after credential deletion, management loads, and account detail updates. Signal failures never undo successful server operations.
+
+The client uses native WebAuthn APIs with `webauthn-polyfills` for JSON serialization and capability detection; SimpleWebAuthn handles server-side validation. Challenges are session-bound, expire after five minutes, and are consumed before verification. Sessions rotate after registration and sign-in. Mutations require an exact Origin match; cookies are HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, and Secure under HTTPS.
+
+Provider hints are a small local snapshot of common AAGUID entries from the [passkeydeveloper registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids), retrieved October 20, 2026. Unknown and zero AAGUIDs use a generic icon. These hints never affect authentication or authorization. To update hints, replace entries in `src/providers.json` and rebuild.
+
+## Automated checks
+
+```sh
+pnpm build
+pnpm test
+```
+
+Tests use a software authenticator with real EC keys, CBOR attestation, and signed assertions against HTTP endpoints. They cover registration, authentication, challenge replay and expiry, incorrect origins and signatures, user-handle checks, session rotation, management ownership, deletion protection, and user verification for reauthentication. No browser verification is required.
+
+Server integration follows the [SimpleWebAuthn server documentation](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server).
